uh hi there steve kaufman here today i  want to talk about a subject that comes  up all the time it's motivation  i think motivation is the number one  most important  uh  element  factor in language learning success  i just finished participating in a  a google plus hangout with uh i don't  know seven or eight other polyglots  people who on the internet  regularly post comments like i do  talking about their experience of  language learning and it was hosted by  david manseray who lives in london  and i'm going to leave a  link to it here in the box underneath  the video and if you are interested  please go and listen it was done using  uh google plus hangout technology we're  still kind of ironing out the best  practices for this  technology things like leaving your  microphone on mute when you're not  speaking  but  here were seven or eight nine people who  are obviously very motivated to learn  languages and we were asked several  questions by david  about the different methods that we use  and what we found useful and not useful  and so forth  um one thing that was interesting was  that um  most of the people answered that  there was one question what techniques  that other people use do you not find  useful or something to that effect and a  number of people said that they don't  find it useful to do shadowing they  don't find anki or space repetition or  flash cards useful so that and yet there  are people who find those things useful  and i think what i took out of that was  that if you are motivated  you will find the learning techniques  that work best for you and it really  doesn't matter whether you flash guard  or use anki or use something else  most of the people there  basically said they like to listen and  read  they like to engage with things that  were meaningful and interesting to them  and i think that's a very important part  of motivation  i think that in many cases the  traditional method of teaching is quite  demotivating because it focuses on the  nuts and bolts of the language  trying to get you to pronounce correctly  at an early stage or trying to get you  to remember  grammatical patterns at an early stage  and because this is extremely difficult  to do  people get frustrated  so but yet i still leaving that aside i  still ask myself for example i've  mentioned before that  immigrants to canada do very poorly at  these government-sponsored  language schools  i would think they basically achieve  you know nothing that they couldn't  achieve  more effectively on their own if they  were motivated  i i would think for a motivated person  the time spent in the classroom is  actually a waste of time because you're  in there listening to 10 15 20 other  people struggle with the language  whereas if you are genuinely motivated  you could learn  using resources on the internet if  you're living in a place where the  language is spoken you're just  surrounded by  uh you know resources that can help you  learn but so few people are motivated  and so i asked myself here we have these  nine  keeners and and then subsequent to that  i had a discussion in chinese with  nicholas from sweden who's 18 years old  and who speaks flawless english spanish  and now has been at chinese he says for  four months and he speaks like wow  extremely well again a very motivated  person  so why is it that some people are so  motivated and other people  uh sort of say yeah like i spoke to a  lady the other day at a chinese  restaurant we were speaking in chinese  and she said yeah you know i've been  here three years and but uh you know and  i really want to learn but uh you know  i'm having a lot of trouble and stuff  and she can hardly speak english  and uh  you know if you were really motivated  like i didn't want to pro but like how  much time do you actually spend  with english how much effort do you put  into it  so that i think there's various levels  of motivation there's all there are  those people who aren't interested at  all  and i knew a lot of those when i lived  in japan for example uh  europeans north americans who lived  there and made absolutely no effort to  get beyond the you know konichiwa in  japanese so there's the zero motivation  group then there's the people who say  yeah i would love to be able to speak  spanish  uh but they don't you know they sort of  at a certain level they're motivated but  they either aren't sufficiently  motivated to push  or they become easily discouraged and  this was another point that was raised  by luca  that the initial period with the  language can be quite discouraging  because it's so difficult you basically  you can't make sense of the language  nothing  you don't understand and stuff  and so it is important to persevere  and  and get break through that to the next  level now in my own experience i find  that the initial period in some ways is  quite rewarding because very quickly you  know the word for dog horse cow  you know some things whereas previously  you knew nothing  but then comes that very long period  where you have to move from  some understanding of the language to  actually reaching a comfortable level of  fluency so i think that intermediate to  advanced stage takes a much longer  period of time  than getting from zero to some  understanding in the language and i  think there too people lose their  motivation  and there again my advice always is to  give yourself credit for more than you  think you know for being better than you  think you are but also stay with  activities that are interesting and  follow your interests now that's  assuming that people have interest if  you have no interest in any aspect then  probably you're not going to be so  motivated but  i just felt coming off that discussion  with those polyglots and also with  nicholas i said there's a small group of  people maybe those are the people who  watch my videos  people who are into learning more than  one language  that's a very small number and then  there's a very large number of people  who  to varying degrees but like to learn a  language  but they aren't motivated enough now why  is that is it that we have to make it  easier for them to achieve some success  that they could feed off that success to  become more motivated  like that's the thing i always struggle  with how do you  you know motivate people  assuming that there's some degree of  interest in speaking another language  you know is it a matter of convincing  them that they'll feel so much happier  if they speak that other language  uh you know there's making the reward  more palpable to them  um  you know i think of um  you know again i i was talking to  someone in in europe and there  unemployed people receive 600 euros a  month but if they attend a language  school they get 800 euros a month so  they're they are motivated to attend the  language school but this person told me  most people don't learn very much there  so they're the motivation drives them to  attend the language school six hours a  day with 20 people in the classroom  uh two so if they do that for a year  that's 1200 euros  um 200 additional what did i say 12 2  400 euros  maybe you could motivate them by saying  if you achieve level x in the language  we will give you 2 400 euros rather than  motivating them to go to school  when in fact the schools are very often  quite unsuccessful at teaching the  language maybe the motivation should be  if you achieve a level x we will give  you 2 400 euros now you still have to  find people willing to come up with the  money to motivate people but even if you  don't get the money the rewards for  being comfortable in the language to me  are so obvious  in terms of professional opportunities  in terms of  cultural enrichment in terms of being  able to make friends and travel in  different countries that it's like  getting a bonus of 2 500 euros or 5 000  euros it's worth more than 5 000 euros  so i guess the question is how do you  make i guess how do you initially make  that  that uh achievement of being comfortable  in another language seem  genuinely attractive to people so that  they're just committed to it and then  how do you maintain their motivation by  making sure that they do activities that  are interesting to them  and then how do you ensure that they  achieve some degree of success so that  that will in turn  encourage them to continue and i guess  part of that is to not allow them to be  discouraged  that allow them to recognize that it  takes a long time to learn a language  so  i mean these are some of the things that  that  you know i often wonder about i mean  potentially i think you know if if  there's a very small percentage of  people who are very motivated motivated  to learn languages how do we make that  10 times the number so i'd be interested  in hearing your comments about this  whole issue  of motivation and how to motivate people  beyond the sort of i call ourselves the  little ghetto of polyglots but into the  sort of average population how do you  get more people interested in learning  languages and get them to stay with the  program until they achieve success so i  look forward to hearing  any comments thank you for listening
 

